    {
      "page_title": "Choosing the Right Data Structure",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Decision Guide",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "Unique items only? → Set",
              "Need key-value lookup? → Dict",
              "Add/remove at both ends? → deque",
              "Is the data fixed? → Tuple",
              "Otherwise → List"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Real-World Scenarios",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Shopping cart items → List (order, dupes)",
            "Point coordinates (x,y) → Tuple (fixed pair)",
            "Remove duplicates from a list → Set",
            "Does this email exist? → Set (fast check)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "User info by id → Dict (lookup by key)",
            "Task queue (FIFO) → deque",
            "Editor undo/redo → List used as a stack",
            "Word frequency in a file → Dict (word → count)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "List the operations you need first, then pick the structure that makes those operations cheap."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "✗ List for membership → O(n); use a set",
            "✗ List as a dict key → error; use tuple or str",
            "✗ Dict when you only want unique values → set",
            "✗ list.pop(0) as a queue → O(n); use deque",
            "✗ Tuple when you must edit → use a list"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Remember",
          "text": "The right structure often turns O(n) into O(1) — the same code, an order of magnitude faster."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "At a Glance",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
